<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Neural Plast</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">NP</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Neural Plasticity</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">2090-5904</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1687-5443</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">16097483</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2565458</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1155/NP.2005.153</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Cerebellar Development and Plasticity: Perspectives for Motor Coordination Strategies, for Motor Skills, and for Therapy</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Swinny</surname><given-names>J. D.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I1"><sup>1, 4</sup></xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I4"/><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1">*</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>van der Want</surname><given-names>J. J. L.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I2"><sup>2</sup></xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Gramsbergen</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="I3"><sup>3</sup></xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="I1"><sup>1</sup>Laboratory for Stress Neurobiology, 409 Abramson Research Center, The Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104, USA</aff><aff id="I2"><sup>2</sup>Laboratory for Cell Biology and Electron Microscopy, University Medical Center Groningen, , The Netherlands</aff><aff id="I3"><sup>3</sup>Laboratory for Medical Physiology, University Medical Center Groningen, , The Netherlands</aff><aff id="I4"><sup>4</sup>Lab for Stress Neurobiology, 409 Abramson Research Center, The Children's Hospital of Philadelphia, Philadelphia, PA 19104-4399, USA</aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1">*J. D. Swinny</corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2005</year></pub-date><volume>12</volume><issue>2-3</issue><fpage>153</fpage><lpage>160</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2005.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2005</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p>The role of the mammalian cerebellum ranges from motor coordination, sensory-motor integration, motor learning, and timing to nonmotor functions such as cognition. In terms of motor function, the development of the cerebellum is of particular interest because animal studies show that the development of the cerebellar cortical circuitry closely parallels motor coordination. Ultrastructural analysis of the morphological development of the cerebellar circuitry, coupled with the temporal and spatial identification of the neurochemical substrates expressed during development, will help to elucidate their roles in the establishment of the cerebellar circuitry and hence motor activity. Furthermore, the convenience of a number of naturally occurring mouse mutations has allowed a functional dissection of the various cellular elements that make up the cerebellar circuitry. This understanding will also help in the approach to possible therapies of pathologies arising during development because tile cerebellum is especially prone to such perturbation because of its late development.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>